+# B7 — Curved KS collapse with production, 2026-09-07
+
+**Outcome: `TURNS_OVER` — for an open set of admissible data in each of closures (b) and (c).** This is an existence result for the authorised curved Kantowski–Sachs (KS) Weyssenhoff FLUID systems. It is not a result for all initial data or a prediction for an unspecified source trajectory. No class is filed and no general cosmological conclusion is drawn.
+
+Curvature can outpace production in the fractional shear–spin comparison, including immediately before a regular turnaround. That does not prevent a turnaround if spin has already reached the required amplitude. The integrations below also exhibit initially subdominant spin overtaking shear during contraction. Neither a positive production coefficient nor the source's printed Eq. (35), by itself, is a sufficient turnaround test.
+
+**Source custody and quotation provenance.** The governing text actually read was the saved [publisher article HTML](b4_springer_access.html), Popławski, *General Relativity and Gravitation* **53**, 18 (2021), DOI 10.1007/s10714-021-02790-7. Its SHA256 is `fb94da36db11823ed1081fd39226a7134dec64883a7811c4011a1ea7234efc88`. I read its MathJax payloads for (1), (6), (10), (11), (14)–(16), (25), and (30)–(37), together with the relevant prose in §§2 and 7–9, by stripping tags from those saved bytes for display. This was my own display of the publisher HTML, not the arXiv copy or a pre-existing extraction. The single verbatim equation quotation below is explicitly labelled the same way. All other displayed equations below are my definitions, rearrangements or derivations, not verbatim quotations. No APS PDF or arXiv copy was needed.
+
+The existing lane records were used as fixed inputs, not reopened: [B2 closure definitions](B2_CLOSURE_20260907.md), [B4 parameter and units record](B4_BETA_COMPARISON_20260907.md), [B6 established KS identity](B6_APPLICABILITY_20260907.md), and [its standing referee qualification](KIMI_B6_CHALLENGE_20260907.md). Their SHA256 values, respectively, are:
+
+```text
+d99f286fc66dfbe1f313c7a7dab5f0c73933f7b8378e7ec96179290e437bd2b7
+f4cf91f797358af603e7c63da30706430f60863d8000668be244927a9537394d
+7531c8edc30ae745f59a4a27190848dd85e9fd5ec0f3fe460fd8adc36d974c59
+9faf751d04b4baa69ee453db401e65dafc252734134790deab192dadc2f1bfbe
+```
+
+No excluded evaluation data or other lane's contents were used. No flat Bianchi I result is used in the derivation or integration. The Dirac row is excluded throughout.
+
+**1. Exact system, separately closed.** Use the source's proper-time convention (c=1), positive (X,Y,n_f,epsilon), and definitions
+
+\[
+u=\dot X/X,\quad v=\dot Y/Y,\quad
+V=XY^2=a^3,\quad H=(u+2v)/3,\quad
+\Delta=u-v,\quad K=Y^{-2}>0,\quad S=\Delta^2/3.
+\]
+
+Here (S) is the source's printed shear scalar (\sigma^2), with its factor (1/2), not its square root. Set (n=n_f) temporarily. Publisher (1) gives (\rho=\epsilon-\alpha n^2), (P=p-\alpha n^2); both B2 closures retain (p=\epsilon/3). The following spatial evolution equations come directly from publisher (10):
+
+\[
+\dot{\log X}=u,\qquad \dot{\log Y}=v,
+\]
+\[
+\dot u=-u^2-uv+\tfrac12(v^2+K-\kappa P),\qquad
+\dot v=-\tfrac12(3v^2+K+\kappa P).
+\tag{B7.1}
+\]
+
+Publisher (14) and (34), with its production prescription, give
+
+\[
+\Psi=\beta H^4,\qquad
+\dot n=\beta H^4-3Hn,\qquad
+\dot\epsilon=2\alpha n\beta H^4-4H\epsilon.
+\tag{B7.2}
+\]
+
+The energy source (2\alpha n\Psi) is required by conservation of the effective stress in this FLUID system. It is retained in both integrations. The constraint imposed on initial data is
+
+\[
+C=v^2+2uv+K-\kappa(\epsilon-\alpha n^2)
+ =3H^2-S+K-\kappa\epsilon+\kappa\alpha n^2=0.
+\tag{B7.3}
+\]
+
+**Closure (b)** integrates the six variables ((\log X,\log Y,u,v,n,T)), with
+
+\[
+\epsilon=h_\star T^4,\qquad
+\dot T=\frac{2\alpha n\beta H^4-4Hh_\star T^4}{4h_\star T^3}.
+\tag{B7.4b}
+\]
+
+**Closure (c)** has (\epsilon=F(T)) for a specified smooth function on a regular branch (F'(T)\ne0). Its temperature equation is
+
+\[
+\dot T=\frac{2\alpha n\beta H^4-4HF(T)}{F'(T)}.
+\tag{B7.4c}
+\]
+
+For its separate run the script uses the exact coordinate (\epsilon=F(T)), integrating ((\log X,\log Y,u,v,n,\epsilon)) via (B7.1)–(B7.2). The chain rule (F'\dot T=\dot\epsilon) removes (F) from the geometry/energy/number dynamics. This is not a new closure or a choice of (F). A temperature trajectory is recovered as (T(t)=F^{-1}(\epsilon(t))) on the specified inverse branch. A numerical row represents a full closure-(c) trajectory only if that branch's image contains the row's entire energy range. No numerical (T(t)) is claimed for an unspecified (F).
+
+This domain qualification does **not** leave the requested open-set existence result dependent on a numerical choice of (F): section 4 proves it for every regular branch containing any positive energy value. If instead a particular temperature history or a particular distant initial datum is requested, its (F), branch and domain would have to be supplied. A zero of (F') or exit from its image is a constitutive-domain boundary, not automatically a spacetime singularity.
+
+Neither run imposes (n=h_{nf}T^3). Publisher (37), derived using that dropped relation, is not an extra equation of either B2 closure. No pressure, temperature or density closure has been added.
+
+**2. Constraint propagation for the exact integrated equations.** Define the effective energy residual
+
+\[
+E=\dot\epsilon-2\alpha n\dot n+3H(4\epsilon/3-2\alpha n^2).
+\]
+
+Substitution of (B7.2) gives (E=0). Differentiating (B7.3), using (B7.1), (\dot K=-2vK), and the relevant closure's own (\dot\epsilon), gives the off-constraint identity
+
+\[
+\boxed{\dot C=-(u+2v)C=-3HC.}
+\tag{B7.5}
+\]
+
+Thus (C(t)=C(t_0)V(t_0)/V(t)); compatible data stay compatible. Small numerical errors can grow during contraction, making the measured residual essential. The system evolves (u,v) across (H=0); it never takes a signed square root of the constraint or resets any variable to project (C) to zero. (K=Y^{-2}) is recomputed from the integrated (Y).
+
+The script symbolically differentiates this actual spatial-equation system and substitutes each closure by the chain rule, obtaining:
+
+```text
+closure_b_energy_residual=0
+closure_b_constraint_propagation_residual=0
+closure_c_energy_residual=0
+closure_c_constraint_propagation_residual=0
+```
+
+One off-constraint detail matters for reproducibility. With
+
+\[
+\mathcal R=-H^2-\frac23S-\frac\kappa3\epsilon
+                       +\frac23\kappa\alpha n^2,
+\]
+
+the chosen spatial equations give (\dot H=\mathcal R-C/6), or equivalently
+
+\[
+\dot H=-2H^2+\frac{\kappa\alpha n^2-S-K}{3}+\frac C6.
+\tag{B7.6}
+\]
+
+The script checks both identities, including the displayed constraint terms. All turning conditions below apply on (C=0).
+
+**3. Explicit turning condition in the source's variables.** A regular volume turnaround has (X,Y>0), finite matter and geometry, and
+
+\[
+\frac{\dot X}{X}+2\frac{\dot Y}{Y}=0.
+\]
+
+It is a contracting-to-expanding crossing precisely when
+
+\[
+\boxed{\kappa\alpha n_f^2>
+\frac13\left(\frac{\dot X}{X}-\frac{\dot Y}{Y}\right)^2
++\frac1{Y^2}
+=\frac{\Delta^2}{3}+K.}
+\tag{B7.7}
+\]
+
+Then (\dot H=(\kappa\alpha n_f^2-S-K)/3>0), and (\ddot a=a\dot H>0) at (H=0). This is a minimum of (a=(XY^2)^{1/3}), not necessarily simultaneous minima of both directional scale factors. In particular, the positive-(\Delta) example below has (\dot X>0) and (\dot Y<0) at the volume minimum.
+
+The source's (\beta) is absent from the **instantaneous** inequality because (\Psi=\beta H^4=0) at the crossing for every finite (\beta). Its accumulated effect is explicit through number balance:
+
+\[
+n_f(t)=\frac{n_{f0}X_0Y_0^2+
+\dfrac\beta{81}\int_{t_0}^{t}XY^2
+\left(\dfrac{\dot X}{X}+2\dfrac{\dot Y}{Y}\right)^4dt'}{XY^2}.
+\]
+
+Consequently (B7.7), at the zero of the volume expansion, is equivalently the following inequality carrying all requested source variables and (\beta):
+
+\[
+\boxed{\kappa\alpha\left[n_{f0}X_0Y_0^2+
+\frac\beta{81}\int_{t_0}^{t_b}XY^2
+\left(\frac{\dot X}{X}+2\frac{\dot Y}{Y}\right)^4dt'\right]^2
+>(X_bY_b^2)^2\left(\frac{\Delta_b^2}{3}+K_b\right).}
+\tag{B7.8}
+\]
+
+This is a trajectory-dependent criterion, not a numerical lower bound on (\beta) obtainable without solving the geometry. Away from (H=0), the stronger condition for (\dot H>0) is
+
+\[
+\kappa\alpha n_f^2>S+K+6H^2
+=\frac{\Delta^2}{3}+K+
+\frac23\left(\frac{\dot X}{X}+2\frac{\dot Y}{Y}\right)^2.
+\tag{B7.9}
+\]
+
+Equality in (B7.7) is not silently counted as a turn. At (H=0), (C=0) and (\kappa\alpha n^2=S+K), differentiating the same smooth system gives (\ddot H=-4K\Delta/9). For (\Delta\ne0), the leading term in (H(t_b+\tau)) is quadratic and there is no sign-changing crossing. For (\Delta=0), (\ddot H=0) and (H^{(3)}=-4K^2/9<0): the cubic crossing is from expansion to contraction. These symbolic boundary checks are in the script. Thus a degenerate equality does not hide a regular contracting-to-expanding crossing in these closures.
+
+**4. Why this is an open-set result, not merely an event found numerically.** Fix any finite (\beta>0), any (\alpha>0), and any positive energy value (\epsilon_b) in the regular domain of the chosen closure. In (b), take (T_b=(\epsilon_b/h_\star)^{1/4}>0); in (c), take a regular (T_b>0) with (F(T_b)=\epsilon_b). Construct a point with
+
+\[
+H_b=0,\quad K_b=\frac{\kappa\epsilon_b}{4},\quad
+S_b=\frac{\kappa\epsilon_b}{12},\quad
+\Delta_b=\frac{\sqrt{\kappa\epsilon_b}}{2}>0,
+\]
+\[
+\kappa\alpha n_b^2=\frac56\kappa\epsilon_b,\quad
+Y_b=\frac2{\sqrt{\kappa\epsilon_b}},\quad X_b>0.
+\tag{B7.10}
+\]
+
+Set (u_b=2\Delta_b/3), (v_b=-\Delta_b/3). Direct substitution gives (C_b=0) and
+
+\[
+\kappa\alpha n_b^2-S_b-K_b=\frac12\kappa\epsilon_b>0,
+\qquad \dot H_b=\frac16\kappa\epsilon_b>0.
+\]
+
+The locally smooth vector field has a unique solution on both sides. For sufficiently small (\eta>0), (H(t_b-\eta)<0) and (H(t_b+\eta)>0), with all physical variables regular and positive. The inverse-temperature branch in (c) remains valid on a sufficiently small neighbourhood. Continuous dependence and the transverse event (\dot H_b>0) give an **open neighbourhood on the constraint manifold** of contracting initial data at (t_b-\eta) whose solutions also turn. The constraint manifold is regular, since its derivative with respect to (\epsilon) is (-\kappa\ne0).
+
+This construction works for every (\alpha\in[\alpha_-,\alpha_+]) with (0<\alpha_-\le\alpha_+<\infty), and for each finite positive (\beta); it does not fit either parameter to a preferred number. On compact parameter intervals the neighbourhood can be made uniform by smooth dependence. It proves existence of open sets, not that a fixed initially weak-spin datum turns for every (\beta>0). It also does not require production to dominate the fractional race near the crossing.
+
+**5. Shear versus spin, and comparison with publisher (35).** The established KS identity is retained exactly. With (N=Vn_f) and (D=V\Delta),
+
+\[
+\dot N=\beta VH^4,\qquad \dot D=VK=X,\qquad
+\frac{d(V^2S)}{dt}=\frac23V^2K\Delta.
+\]
+
+For (n_f>0), (\Delta\ne0), the actual dimensionless spin/shear ratio obeys
+
+\[
+R=\frac{\kappa\alpha n_f^2}{S}
+=\frac{3\kappa\alpha N^2}{D^2},\qquad
+\boxed{\frac{\dot R}{R}
+=2\left(\frac{\beta H^4}{n_f}-\frac K\Delta\right).}
+\tag{B7.11}
+\]
+
+For the positive-(\Delta) branch, shear outpaces spin fractionally if (\beta H^4/n_f<K/\Delta); production makes spin gain fractionally if the inequality reverses. In source variables the latter condition is
+
+\[
+\frac{\beta}{81n_f}\left(\frac{\dot X}{X}
++2\frac{\dot Y}{Y}\right)^4>\frac{1}{Y^2\Delta},
+\qquad \Delta>0.
+\tag{B7.12}
+\]
+
+For (\Delta<0), both terms in (B7.11) increase (R) when (\beta\ge0); this alone does not establish a future turn. At (\Delta=0), (R) is undefined, while the signed equation (\dot\Delta+3H\Delta=K) stays regular. During contraction on (\Delta>0), both shear and spin can grow; the sign in (B7.11) compares their fractional growth, not whether either decreases absolutely.
+
+**Verbatim quotation — publisher HTML, MathJax payload `Equ35`, read from the saved publisher bytes using my own tag-stripping display; not arXiv or a prior extraction:**
+
+> \[\begin{aligned}
+> \frac{2\beta n_{\text {f}}}{81}\Bigl (\frac{\dot{X}}{X} +\frac{2\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr )^{4}>\frac{2}{3Y^{2}}\Bigl (\frac{\dot{X}}{X} -\frac{\dot{Y}}{Y}\Bigr ).
+> \end{aligned}\]
+> (35)
+
+Printed (35) is (2\beta n_fH^4>(2/3)K\Delta). It compares **absolute source terms** in the (n_f^2) and (S) evolution equations, after moving their dilution terms to the left. It is not the fractional-rate condition (B7.12), and neither is the amplitude-and-constraint turning condition (B7.7). These are **different conditions**. Even as a formal numerical comparison, (35) and (B7.12) have equal thresholds only where (n_f^2=S=\Delta^2/3), not generically. In physical source units these unweighted quantities have different dimensions: the two sides of printed (35) have dimensions (L^{-7}) and (L^{-3}). No number-density rescaling is printed to resolve that issue. I preserve (35), rather than silently insert (\kappa\alpha) or use it to calibrate (\beta).
+
+For the properly weighted *comoving difference* one instead has
+
+\[
+\frac{d\{V^2(\kappa\alpha n_f^2-S)\}}{dt}
+=V^2\left(2\kappa\alpha\beta n_fH^4-\frac23K\Delta\right).
+\]
+
+This too is distinct from both a fractional comparison and a turning criterion. At a positive-(\Delta) turn, (H=0), so (\dot R/R=-2K/\Delta<0) and printed (35) fails, while (B7.7) can hold strictly. The open-set construction and the numerical example both realise this situation.
+
+Comparison with source (32) is also precise. Define (J=2\kappa\alpha n_f^2-2S-\kappa\epsilon), the margin in its printed inequality. On the constraint, (J=3(\dot H+H^2)). Thus (32) agrees with (B7.7) **at (H=0)**; away from that surface it is an acceleration condition (\ddot a>0), weaker than (B7.9). This local identity is not a singularity-avoidance theorem. The exact persistence equation in either closure is
+
+\[
+\dot J=-6HJ-2H\kappa\epsilon
++2\kappa\alpha\beta n_fH^4-\frac43K\Delta.
+\tag{B7.13}
+\]
+
+At (J=0), the sign depends on all three remaining terms, including the energy evolution. Printed (35) is not that boundary derivative condition. This calculation makes the distinction without revising B6.
+
+**6. Units, parameter interval and deterministic integrations.** The script uses dimensionless coordinates, explicitly distinct from physical source parameter values. Choose a length/time unit (L_0>0), an independent number-density unit (n_0^{\rm ref}>0), and (\epsilon_0^{\rm ref}=1/(\kappa L_0^2)). Define
+
+\[
+\hat t=t/L_0,\quad \hat X=X/L_0,\quad \hat Y=Y/L_0,
+\quad \hat H=L_0H,\quad \hat K=L_0^2K,
+\quad e=\epsilon/\epsilon_0^{\rm ref},\quad q=n_f/n_0^{\rm ref},
+\]
+\[
+A=\alpha(n_0^{\rm ref})^2/\epsilon_0^{\rm ref},\qquad
+B=\beta/(n_0^{\rm ref}L_0^3).
+\tag{B7.14}
+\]
+
+These make (\kappa=1) in the numerical equations, with (A,B) in place of physical (\alpha,\beta). Closure (b) uses (T_0=(\epsilon_0^{\rm ref}/h_\star)^{1/4}), so (e=\hat T^4). Closure (c) uses the energy coordinate directly. No source constant is declared numerically equal to one in physical units.
+
+Carry the inherited **exploratory dimensionless interval** (A\in[0.25,1]), sampled at (0.25,0.5,1); these endpoints are not a source-derived uncertainty estimate. The symbolic results retain the whole positive (\alpha) interval. Sample (B=0.01,0.1,1) as parameters, not inferred production rates. Physical values would be (\beta=B n_0^{\rm ref}L_0^3), whose normalization and value are not fixed by the source. No value has been selected as the answer. Holding (w=Aq^2) fixed while varying (A) changes the initial (q); this is the parameterized family actually tested.
+
+The main contracting family starts at
+
+\[
+\hat X_0=\hat Y_0=1,\quad \hat H_0=-1,\quad
+\hat\Delta_0=3,\quad \hat K_0=1,\quad
+w_0=Aq_0^2=0.1,\quad e_0=1.1.
+\]
+
+Thus (u_0=1,v_0=-2), (R_0=1/30), and (C_0=0). These are curved, anisotropic, initially shear-dominated admissible data. They are illustrative initial data, not a claimed numerical extraction of the source's mass or horizon data.
